  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Cool; lake effect rain showers diminish today, with \nafternoon convection lingering east of Lake Ontario into the early \nevening.\n\nA chilly airmass to start the day today, with 850 hPa temperatures \naround +7 to +8C over the warm lake waters aiding in lake effect \nrain showers. As drier air approaches activity over and off Lake \nErie will continue to weaken, while a still moderate, but narrow \nplume of lake effect rain east and southeast of Lake Ontario will \ncontinue just past sunrise. As drier air and daytime increased \nmixing heights weaken the organization of the lake effect rain band \nexpect this band of rain to weaken to scattered showers by late \nafternoon. \n\nAn upper level trough axis will pass across eastern zones this \nafternoon, and this feature combined with very limited daytime \ninstability will trigger a few showers and isolated thunderstorms \nthis afternoon. Limited shear will likely leave this activity as \njust garden variety storms that will end through the late afternoon \nand early evening with the loss of daytime heating and now on the \nbackside of the recently passed mid level trough axis. \n\nKey Message 2...Showers and thunderstorms arrive Thursday and last \nthrough Friday.\n\nA shortwave trough will guide a cold front towards our region \nThursday...with this front nearly stalling across our region through \nFriday morning as an upper level low deepens and slides southward \nfrom Hudson Bay. A southerly flow of moisture ahead of this front \nwill boost PWat values upwards towards 1.5 inches, that with an \nincreasing warm cloud depth to 11K-12K feet and short MBE vectors \ncould bring a period of moderate rain showers and thunderstorms. Any \ntraining of storms along this stalling front could pose a risk for \nat least poor drainage flooding. A weaker wind flow aloft leading to \n0-6 km bulk shear vectors less than 30 knots suggests the overall \nsevere threat will remain limited. \n\nThis front will then push eastward later Friday as surface high \npressure from the northern Plains expands eastward, with a drier \nairmass diminishing the rain showers.",
